Q: How to write a hyperlink to a controller method with a parameter in CodeIgniter A: <p>there is an error in your View file : missing <code>/</code> in delete url</p>

<p>your code :</p>

<pre><code>&lt;a class="btn btn-danger" href="&lt;?php echo site_url('PdgDashboard/deleteEmployee'.$new_Employee['name_Employee']);  ?&gt;"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>correct code is :</p>

<pre><code>&lt;a class="btn btn-danger" href="&lt;?php echo site_url('PdgDashboard/deleteEmployee/'.$new_Employee['name_Employee']);  ?&gt;"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>add <code>/</code> in your link after delete function </p>

<pre><code>site_url('PdgDashboard/deleteEmployee/'.$new_Employee['name_Employee']);
</code></pre>

Q: In Vitest with Angular, How to fix Compilation error &#39;&#39;Can&#39;t bind to &#39;myProperty&#39; since it isn&#39;t a known property of &#39;app-a&#39; for running tests? A: <p>This is an angular compilation error and not a vitest issue.</p>
<p>Angular can find <code>&lt;app-a&gt;</code> but it cannot find an input named <code>myProperty</code>. Make sure <code>myProperty</code> is declared as an input on <code>AComponent</code>.</p>
<p>Angular 21 components are standalone by default. Since your test component and mocks are under declarations they need <code>standalone: false</code></p>
<p>Importing the real module works because it is probably declaring or importing something this test is missing. Add that specific component or directive instead of importing the entire module.</p>
<p>Do not use <code>NO_ERRORS_SCHEMA</code> or <code>CUSTOM_ELEMENTS_SCHEMA</code>. That only hides the error.</p>
<pre><code>import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';
import { Component, ViewChild } from '@angular/core';
import { ComponentFixture, TestBed } from '@angular/core/testing';

import { AComponent } from './a.component';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-b',
  standalone: false,
  template: ''
})
class MockBComponent {}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-c',
  standalone: false,
  template: ''
})
class MockCComponent {}

@Component({
  standalone: false,
  template: `
    &lt;app-a #acomp [myProperty]=&quot;testVal&quot;&gt;
      &lt;app-b&gt;&lt;/app-b&gt;
      &lt;app-c&gt;&lt;/app-c&gt;
    &lt;/app-a&gt;
  `
})
class TestComponent {
  @ViewChild('acomp') aComponent!: AComponent;
  testVal = 'hello';
}

describe('AComponent UT', () =&gt; {
  let component: TestComponent;
  let fixture: ComponentFixture&lt;TestComponent&gt;;

  beforeEach(async () =&gt; {
    await TestBed.configureTestingModule({
      imports: [CommonModule],
      declarations: [
        TestComponent,
        AComponent,
        MockBComponent,
        MockCComponent
      ]
    }).compileComponents();

    fixture = TestBed.createComponent(TestComponent);
    component = fixture.componentInstance;
    fixture.detectChanges();
  });

  it('should get rendered', () =&gt; {
    expect(component).toBeDefined();
    expect(component.aComponent).toBeDefined();
  });
});
</code></pre>
<pre><code>import { Component, Input } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
 selector: 'app-a',
 standalone: false,
 templateUrl: './a.component.html'
})
export class AComponent {
  @Input() myProperty!: string;
}
</code></pre>
<p>If <code>myProperty</code> belongs to a directive instead of <code>AComponent</code> add that directive to the test.</p>

Q: help with php controller function A: <ol>
<li>A controller method should not be directly interacting with the database.</li>
<li>CodeIgniter's core <code>get()</code> query builder method does not receive an array as its second parameter; the second parameter is a <code>limit</code> parameter.</li>
<li>Even for query builder methods which accept an array, none of the query builder methods accept an array where the associative keys represent the clause of the query being built.</li>
<li>Unless you have an undisclosed wrapper for CodeIgniter's db class, that <code>get()</code> call will be one of the break points of your scripting.</li>
</ol>
<p>Your controller <code>index()</code> should be loading a <code>Collective_model</code>, then calling an indicatively named method to acquire the sought data set.</p>
<p>Controller:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>class Spoonful extends CI_Controller
{
    public function __construct()
    {
        parent::__construct();
    }

    public function index()
    {
        $this-&gt;load-&gt;model('Collective_model', 'CollectiveModel');
        $data['collectives'] = $this-&gt;CollectiveModel-&gt;getLatestForMailchimp();
        $this-&gt;load-&gt;view('spoonful', $data);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Do not return <code>null</code> from a method which otherwise returns an array of zero or more arrays/objects. This way your view loop doesn't need to check if it is dealing with an array.</p>
<p>Model:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>class Property_model extends CI_Model
{
    public function __construct()
    {
        parent::__construct();
    }

    public function getLatestForMailchimp(?int $limit = 3): array
    {
        return $this-&gt;db
            -&gt;select('c.*, m.username, i.path avatar')
            -&gt;join('member m',          'm.member_id=c.member_id')
            -&gt;join('collective_tag ct', 'ct.collective_id=c.collective_id')
            -&gt;join('tag t',             't.tag_id=ct.tag_id')
            -&gt;join('image i',           'i.image_id=m.avatar_id')
            -&gt;order_by('c.added', 'desc')
            -&gt;get_where('collective c', ['t.value' =&gt; 'mailchimp'], $limit)
            -&gt;result_array();
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Entity Framework Core self referencing many-to-many relationship A: <p>The issue is that you cannot map two different relationships to the same navigation property (BundleProducts)  If you want bi-directional navigation properties, you need separate collections.  Think of it this way with the Product.BundleProducts:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public ICollection&lt;ProductBundle&gt; ProductsIAmBundledUnder { get; private set; } = [];
public ICollection&lt;ProductBundle&gt; ProductsBundledUnderMe {get; private set; } = [];
</code></pre>
<p>Where under ProductBundle, the &quot;ProductId&quot; links to ProductsIAmBundledUnder, and &quot;BundleId&quot; links to ProductsBundledUnderMe.</p>
<p>A PerfumeBottle might only exist within one or more bundle like a particular box Set, or  gift set so only exist in ProductBundle by ProductId,  but a box set could be itself bundled under a gift set. Existing in some cases as a BundleId as well as a ProductId within the relationships. If Product only has a single collection, how could it represent both the collection of products it contains and the products it is part of?</p>

Q: codeigniter using function inside a controller A: <p>The only thing that you need to do is call your recursive function AFTER declaring it. Here's proof that moving the position of function call solves the issue: <a href="https://3v4l.org/6tvcA#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://3v4l.org/6tvcA#v</a></p>
<p>The fact that your function is called before it is declared within the context of a method is <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/4637947/2943403">one of those exceptions</a>. See example 3 at <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/functions.user-defined.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.php.net/manual/en/functions.user-defined.php</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function home()
{
    $num = 1;
    function change($num, $rand = false) {
        echo 'inside function';
        if ($num &lt; 4) {
            $rand = rand(1, 9);
            $num = $num . $rand;
            change($num, $rand);
        } else {
            echo $num;
        }
    }
    change($num);
    echo 'done';
}
</code></pre>
<p>That said, declaring and calling a function inside of a class method isn't very <em>OOP</em>. I don't understand the point of the <code>$rand</code> parameter. Regardless of the passed in value of <code>$rand</code>, that value will either be overwritten by a random value between 1 and 9 or completely ignored. Also, the <code>$rand</code> number is always appended to the <code>$num</code> value, so this function acts as a concatenating tool - I hope that was your intent.</p>
<p>While this is likely just a coding experimentation, it is advisable to not print data from controllers (with the exception of json payloads that don't benefit from a view file). <a href="https://3v4l.org/rSnd1#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>class Test extends CI_Controller
{

    public function __construct()
    {
        parent::__construct();
    }

    public function home()
    {
        $this-&gt;change(1);
        echo 'done';
    }

    private function change($num, $rand = false)
    {
        echo 'inside function';
        if ($num &lt; 4) {
            $rand = rand(1, 9);
            $num = $num . $rand;
            $this-&gt;change($num, $rand);
        } else {
            echo $num;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
